For the string w1w2…wn, consisting of characters 0 and 1, we define f(w) as the number of permutations p1,p2,…,pn of the array [0,1,…,n−1], such that for all i from 1 to n the following holds:if wi=1, then there exist such 1≤l≤r≤n that mex([pl,pl+1,…,pr])=i;∗if wi=0, then there do not exist such 1≤l≤r≤n that mex([pl,pl+1,…,pr])=i.
Given a string s1s2…sn, consisting of characters 0 and 1, and a positive integer c. Note that in this version of the problem, the string s doesn't contain ?. Consider all strings w that can be obtained from s by replacing all characters ? with characters 0 and 1. Find the smallest value of f(w) among all such strings w that is not divisible by c, or determine that such a string w does not exist. Since the answer may be large, find it modulo 109+7.∗
The minimum excluded (MEX) of a collection of integers c1,c2,…,ck is defined as the smallest non-negative integer x which does not occur in the collection c.

Input:
Each test contains multiple test cases. The first line contains the number of test cases t (1≤t≤104). The description of the test cases follows.
The first line of each test case contains two integers n and c (3≤n≤2⋅105, 1≤c≤109) — the length of the string and the number that limits the value of the function.
The second line of each test case contains a string of length n, consisting of characters 0 and 1 — the string s.
It is guaranteed that the sum of n across all test cases does not exceed 2⋅105.

Output:
For each test case, if there exists such a string w that can be obtained from s by replacing all characters ? with characters 0 and 1, such that f(w) is not divisible by c, then output the minimum value of f(w) among all such strings w. The answer should be output modulo 109+7. If such a string does not exist, output −1.

Strategy:
We have 2 types of numbers. Either the MEX for some sequence in the array we create must equal that number or it must not. For the mex to not equal a number we just need to make sure that for any sequence that would have 1 to number, number would be also inside it and it can't be at the edges. If the number has a 1 meaning we have to have a contiguous subsequence that has mex number we only have two choices, we either place the number at the start of the sequence that contains 1 to number-1 or at the end. This way, we guarantee the sequence with mex number will always exist. Therefore, the full solution goes through the input array from 1 to n-1 and checks the digits, if it is zero the multiplier is i-1 since we can place the number anywhere in the current array, in-between any 2 numbers and the mex number will not exist since the sequence that would have it also have the number itself. If the digit is 1 we multiply by 2. This is because our current array has number 1 to i-1. We need to make sure MEX=i exists, therefore i must be on the edges so the sequence is preserved. We go like this for the whole input array. The only exception is the first index for which the only option is to just place the number in thats why we start the loop at i=1. The 2 edge cases are if nums[0] or nums[n-1]=='0'. If this is true there is no solution since the mex=1 will always exist since we can take subsequence 0 and mex=n will also always exist since we take the whole array as the sequence. Since the answer can be big we use modulo on all calculations. Since we also need to make sure the count is not divisible by c and we are applying modulo operations we will do all of them twice, once with %1e9+7 and the second with %c. The first mod gives us the actual answer and %c tells us if the number is not divisible by c. We check output_c for being 0, if it is we output -1, otherwise we output ans. We use long long for everything.